[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The workspace holds, defers, batches and recovers, under contract

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-SCRIBE-WORKSPACE-CONTRACTS
TITLE: The workspace holds, defers, batches and recovers, under contract
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: dev/scripts/test_scribe_workspace.py against an isolated 374-node corpus copy; strictdoc 0.28.3; 2026-08-31
STATEMENT: >>>
Seven contracts pass against an isolated copy of the 374-node canon, each in
its own temporary corpus so a rollback assertion cannot be satisfied by the
working tree being untouched anyway.

They cover: a repeated read reuses the generation object rather than
reloading; a write defers its rebuild and the next read pays it once; a batch
of five writes costs ONE rebuild; the cheap rebuild still catches a duplicate
UID across two documents, with the unmodified corpus as its positive control;
an unparseable write restores the bytes AND drops the graph, after which the
workspace re-derives from disk and serves correctly; resolves() answers
without reconciling; and a failed rebuild leaves the previous generation
serving rather than stranding the workspace.

The batch contract FAILED on the first run and that was the point of writing
it: write() reached the graph through current(), which reconciles, so the
second write of a batch paid a rebuild and the deferral bought nothing. The
fix is a held-graph accessor that does not reconcile -- a mutation edits nodes
in memory and does not need the cross-document view a read needs.

Per-contract wall time is 3.3 to 4.0 s and is almost entirely the cold load of
a freshly copied corpus with no pickle cache; it is not the cost of the
operations under test.
<<<
